diagnosis_text:
A full examination of all relevant subsystems — BGP control plane, data plane, interface state, firewall rules, and traffic-shaping rules — reveals no fault in this network. The eBGP session between router1 (AS 1) and router2 (AS 2) has been continuously established since the FRR daemons started. Both routers report "Connections established: 1, dropped: 0" with no BGP NOTIFICATION messages sent or received in either direction. Keepalive exchanges are occurring precisely on the 60-second interval with the default 180-second hold timer. All interfaces are UP with correct IP assignments, the BGP-learned routes are installed in both routing tables (router1 holds B>* 200.1.1.0/24, router2 holds B>* 195.11.14.0/24), and a 10-packet ping from pc1 (195.11.14.2) to pc2 (200.1.1.2) completes with 0% loss and sub-2 ms RTT. There are no tc netem rules, no iptables blocks, and no custom BGP timers on either device. The only noteworthy observation is that both routers have `debug bgp keepalives` and `debug bgp updates in/out` saved to their startup frr.conf — these generate verbose logging and a marginal CPU overhead, but they are not fault-causing in a two-peer lab. The user's premise that "the eBGP peering keeps flapping" is not supported by any evidence in the live lab. No corrective action is required; the ticket should be closed as a false alarm and the user's connectivity perception re-investigated at the application layer.
=== END ===